<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shadhili Order</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded 13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Shadhiliyya, Shadhili</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shadhili Order is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order (tariqah) founded by Abu al-Hasan al-Shadhili in the 13th century, historically important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and followed today by millions of people around the world. Its writers include Ibn Ata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> al-Iskandari, author of the Hikam, and the commentators Ahmad Zarruq and Ahmad ibn Ajiba, and many head lecturers of al-Azhar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> have followed it. Its branches include the Fassiyya, widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, the Darqawiyya, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in the late 18th century, and the Alawi-Darqawiyya of Ahmad al-Alawi, which spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and English-speaking communities.</p></div>
